The shoulder is the most mobile joint in the body, and for that reason the most delicate. It holds together bones, tendons and a capsule in a balance that breaks easily: through trauma, through wear, through years of overhead work.

Here are the conditions I treat most often. These are not textbook entries: they are how I would explain them to you in clinic.

# Rotator cuff

> Not every tear needs surgery.

The cuff is the group of four tendons that move and centre the humeral head. It tears through trauma or, more often, through age-related degeneration. Night pain, difficulty lifting the arm, loss of strength.

A large share is managed with targeted physiotherapy and injections. When the tear is traumatic, or pain and weakness persist, arthroscopic repair reattaches the tendon to the bone. For **irreparable** tears there are alternatives — tendon transfers, capsular reconstruction, reverse replacement — and choosing well between them is my daily work.

# Instability and dislocation

> Each episode wears away some bone.

A shoulder that comes out once tends to come out again, especially in young people and athletes. The wear is on the glenoid and on the humeral head (Hill-Sachs lesion).

Arthroscopic stabilisation (Bankart, with or without remplissage) restores the labrum and the capsule. When bone loss is significant, bone-adding procedures are needed. The choice depends on how much bone is missing, on age and on the sport.

# Arthritis and replacement

> Anatomic when the cuff works, reverse when it no longer does.

When the cartilage is worn and pain limits daily life, a shoulder replacement restores movement and rest. An anatomic replacement rebuilds the original shape. A reverse replacement is designed for shoulders in which the cuff no longer works.

I use 3D planning and, where useful, patient-specific guides to position the components. The hospital stay is short and rehabilitation starts immediately.

# Fractures

> Many heal without surgery.

Proximal humerus fractures are common after sixty and in sports injuries. When the fragments are displaced, the choice is between plate-and-screw fixation and replacement, depending on age, bone quality and fracture pattern.

